00:07:09.725 --> 00:07:11.350
KRISTIN COPELAND:
Marisa, why don't you

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:13.420
tell us a little
bit about what is

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:15.940
your favorite thing about
being a developer at Google?

00:07:17.915 --> 00:07:19.290
MARISA CHOW: It's
a vivid cliche.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:21.150
There are a lot
of things I really

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:23.240
enjoy about being a developer.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:27.390
And I mean, right off
the bat, my teammates

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.910
that I have are just
really incredible.

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:33.250
I came to Google basically
fresh out of college.

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:36.240
And so I was moving
from an environment

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.290
where coding was
very theoretical.

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:41.400
The problems that we had were
all theoretical in nature.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.710
OK, so this is
what it might look

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:45.935
like to deal with
concurrency problems.

00:07:45.935 --> 00:07:47.310
But then when you
come to Google,

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:50.160
you are suddenly thrown into
this environment where you're

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:56.460
not only forced to see how the
theoretical principles play out

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:59.580
in practice, but also,
you're challenged

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.520
by so many different factors
that you don't really

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.190
think about when you're in
the vacuum of university.

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:09.000
So for example, out
video conferencing fleet

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.180
is all throughout the world.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:11.555
So we deal with
a lot of problems

00:08:11.555 --> 00:08:15.690
around, for example,
time zone issues,

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:21.960
or problems of
different hardware

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:25.980
available in
different countries.

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:29.350
All of the problems
of scale, as well.

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:32.760
For example, if 1% of our
videoconferencing fleet

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.090
goes down, that's hundreds of
rooms that can't be managed,

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:39.947
or can't be used at all by
Googlers in a certain office

00:08:39.947 --> 00:08:40.780
or around the world.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:48.090
So thinking about how the
theories of university days

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:50.790
can--

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:54.090
are put into practice,
I guess, and tested

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:57.600
in many different ways in
the daily developer workflow

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.160
has been very--

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:01.740
it's been such a growing
experience for me

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:04.590
as a developer to see
how that plays out.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:06.300
To think about scale
from the beginning,

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:09.210
to think about the
user from the start.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:10.200
Yeah.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.700
KRISTIN COPELAND:
That's amazing.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.075
And what about from the product
management side, Lakshmi?

00:09:14.075 --> 00:09:16.980
Tell us a little bit more
about what your favorite thing

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:19.380
about being a PM at Google is.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:22.800
LAKSHMI SHARMA: So we say
that PM and Google product

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:25.320
management of Google is
the CEO of their product.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:28.890
And in it, we truly
believe in that sense.

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:32.250
Like you are a CEO off a
product and all that startup

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:33.300
that you run.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:36.990
And then you run with your
CTOs and other architects

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:39.390
of the company, which are
all your engineering leaders.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:41.100
So it's this definition.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:43.940
I've been a leader in many
companies and various places.

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:47.120
It does not practice
to that extent

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:48.580
and to that scale and impact.

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.320
And at least in places
where I have been.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:54.650
So it's just awesome that you
can just think of an idea,

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:56.798
then you just meet an
engineering leader like meet,

00:09:56.798 --> 00:09:57.840
just sitting next to you.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.300
And then say, how about
we work together on this?

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:03.510
Then you go and find a
customer, or you go and find

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:05.500
your open source partners.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:06.390
And if it works out.

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:10.930
But what works out for me, we
always think like global scale.

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:13.080
So nothing starts
regionally for us.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.300
We always start at
will this affect,

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.910
will this problem be solved
for everybody around the world?

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:23.400
And then if it does, we just
go and ask for more people.

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:24.960
We put a business case together.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.240
And sometimes that
idea does not exist.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:28.960
That product never existed.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.290
It's truly as simple as that.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:33.210
I've been here 15
months, and there are so

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:36.240
many new things I've done
in all kinds of aspects.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.520
Partnerships, business
temp, you name it.

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:40.650
And then you just
find the right people.

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:42.540
And there are amazing
sort of people.

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:45.470
So it is just rocking
awesome to be--

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:48.210
and I'm not just
doing a sales pitch.

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:49.320
I truly mean it.

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:51.260
So it's really, really
wonderful to be here.

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:53.010
KRISTIN COPELAND:
Incredible opportunities

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:55.240
to make an impact.

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:56.880
I think one of my
favorite stories--

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:03.150
I was asking a Googler,
what's your Google moment?

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:09.060
And he shares with me that he
was a Newgler, a new Googler,

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:11.270
as we call it.

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:16.110
And he is working on this
business critical project.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:18.180
And it's essentially
when you do a Google

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:21.060
search and the little cards
show up to quickly give you

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:24.990
your snapshot of your answer.

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:29.790
He was working on that, and a
few months after they launched,

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:32.040
the team got a random
email forwarded to them

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:37.050
from a woman in India who noted
that she was able to quickly do

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:38.760
a quick search on
some of her symptoms

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:41.350
that she was having,
quickly diagnosed herself.

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:44.130
Went in and advocated for
herself to get the right care,

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:46.890
and ended up saving both her
life, and she was pregnant,

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:48.190
and her baby's life.

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:51.870
So I think it's just incredible,
these like crazy ways

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:54.480
that we can make just
an impact with something

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.850
that feels like it may not.

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:01.170
Do any of you have sort
of a Google moment?

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:03.600
Or something that's
really just helped you

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:07.620
to feel connected to Google
and just love the magic moments

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:10.390
that we have here?

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:10.890
I know.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.330
This one wasn't on the script.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.310
I'm throwing it at
you a little bit.

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:17.420
TARA HERNANDEZ: [INAUDIBLE]
with this one, Kristin.

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:17.920
I have one.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:20.950
When I was a Newgler,
you spend three days down

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:23.050
in the main campus
learning about Google,

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:25.480
and learning the
tools, and et cetera.

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:27.880
But one of the things I did
not know when I got to Google

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:31.180
was this cool story
out of the Arab Spring,

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:33.880
where you have all of
these activists trying

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:37.510
to advocate and support
democracy in their countries.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:40.147
And in many cases, you
have these very repressive

00:12:40.147 --> 00:12:41.980
governments trying to
shut down the internet

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:45.050
to prevent them from
communicating with each other.

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:46.510
And one of the
things that came out

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:49.540
is that people were spray
painting Google's DNS server

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:53.200
address, which is 8888
on walls of buildings

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:55.000
so that they could keep
their Twitter feeds

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.370
going and various
other social media.

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:00.080
And having not known
that, I was like, wow.

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:02.410
OK, I work at a cool company.

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:04.180
That was pretty awesome to me.

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:09.010
And to consider I
had said I'd never

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:11.470
work at Google, because Google
is that stupid company that

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:15.070
makes you calculate how many
golf balls are in a school bus

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:17.570
or something as part of
your interview process.

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:19.970
Which I can assert
they no longer do.

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:22.690
But to be able to be part
of an organization that

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:25.660
has had that much of a
significant positive impact

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:29.020
in the world in such
a meaningful way.

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:30.733
It's kind of hard to
top that in my mind.

00:13:30.733 --> 00:13:31.900
KRISTIN COPELAND: I love it.

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:33.525
TARA HERNANDEZ:
Except, I'm sure you're

00:13:33.525 --> 00:13:34.600
going to top that, right?

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:35.725
JIMMY MKUDE: No, not quite.

00:13:35.725 --> 00:13:37.270
But that is quite hard.

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:41.320
But when I realized
this is where

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:42.690
I was working on a new project.

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:43.470
It's a new API.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:45.460
We have an ADL implementing it.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:47.810
And everyone's excited about it.

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:49.000
And we want to launch it.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:53.890
So I was talking to one of my
senior engineers in a team,

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:56.483
and I'm like, yeah, right now
the servers are pretty good.

00:13:56.483 --> 00:13:57.400
They're pretty stable.

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:59.860
I can guarantee you like
99% of the time they're

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:00.890
going to be up.

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:01.640
And he's like, no.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:02.492
That's not enough.

00:14:02.492 --> 00:14:03.200
What do you mean?

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:04.710
99% percent of the
time is not enough?

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:05.377
He's like, yeah.

00:14:05.377 --> 00:14:10.180
We need 99.999%
percent of the time.

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:13.990
That 1% can bring down
a lot of-- can affect

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:16.600
a lot of users in one second.

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:19.430
And then he went ahead
and gave it this equation

00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:23.920
that I calculated how many
users we have, just our team.

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:27.910
And how much 10
minutes of one server

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:30.820
going down could
affect the ultimate--

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:32.150
that number just blew my mind.

00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:33.490
I was like, OK.

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:35.830
Yeah, this is big scale.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:37.070
KRISTIN COPELAND: Absolutely.

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:37.570
All right.

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:39.070
So we talked a
little bit about what

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:41.890
some of your favorite things
about being a developer

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:44.380
or product manager or
working at Google are.

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:46.960
Let's hear a little bit
more about that real talk,

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:48.340
like peek under the tent.

00:14:48.340 --> 00:14:50.185
What are your least
favorite things?

00:14:50.185 --> 00:14:52.060
LAKSHMI SHARMA: Can I
say the good one first?

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:52.750
KRISTIN COPELAND: Oh, of course.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:53.542
LAKSHMI SHARMA: OK.

00:14:53.542 --> 00:14:57.940
So I'm infrastructure, so I only
talk infrastructure language.

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:02.030
And if you had seen the keynote
this morning, and I saw, to me,

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:03.460
one of the most
powerful thing was

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:06.400
like imagining putting a
firewood under the ocean.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:08.897
And instead of just,
I've always been

00:15:08.897 --> 00:15:11.230
in companies which will do
software innovation, hardware

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:13.830
innovation, [INAUDIBLE],,
all kinds of innovation.

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:18.160
But OK, so we have a very
high latency between Australia

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:19.280
and Singapore.

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:20.230
So how do we fix it?

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:21.640
We did all the wonderful things.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:23.350
Let's just go to fiber.

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:24.880
And then we reduce the latency.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:26.980
Oh my God.

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:29.680
Now moments like
that, I think just

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:31.690
you have every single
innovation out there,

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:35.110
and then you do something like
the company of a Google scale

00:15:35.110 --> 00:15:37.190
can offer to the world.

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:39.313
I think those are
my aha moments.

00:15:39.313 --> 00:15:40.480
KRISTIN COPELAND: Top notch.

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:41.920
Absolutely.

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:42.490
All right.

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:43.540
Back to the real talk.

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:46.540
JIMMY MKUDE: All right, so one
of my least favorite things

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:50.380
actually relates to
my favorite moment,

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:52.210
as well, which is kind of crazy.

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:55.630
But in that same kind
of example that I just

00:15:55.630 --> 00:16:00.280
told you guys is, because
Google is such a big company,

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:02.890
sometimes it hinders the speed
that you want to do things at.

00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:05.480
So let's say you're
working on a project

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:06.700
and you have everything done.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:09.120
If you're in a
smaller company, you

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:12.970
could get it out pretty
fast and iterate faster.

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:16.600
But sometimes, because Google
is still a very big company,

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:19.930
they really need to--
everyone needs be confident,

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:22.752
and the quality has to be
the highest quality possible.

00:16:22.752 --> 00:16:24.460
So it does take a
little bit longer time,

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:27.730
which as a developer, you
just want to get it done.

00:16:27.730 --> 00:16:31.720
I want people to be able to be
helped by what I've just done.

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:34.510
And it just really hurts to have
to wait like another quarter

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:37.570
and just slowly go to the
reviews and everything

00:16:37.570 --> 00:16:39.350
until it's ready to launch.

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:41.292
So yeah, that's my kind of--

00:16:41.292 --> 00:16:42.250
KRISTIN COPELAND: Yeah.

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:45.070
A lot of trade-offs when
you're worried about scale

00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:47.230
and high impact, you
got to also make sure

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:49.150
that it's not adversely
impacting folks.

00:16:49.150 --> 00:16:51.230
Which means things can
move a little slower.

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:51.940
I get that.

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:52.710
OK.

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:53.883
Marisa?

00:16:53.883 --> 00:16:55.300
MARISA CHOW: Yeah,
I think related

00:16:55.300 --> 00:17:01.150
to Jimmy's point about the
scale at which Google has grown.

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:03.460
As Tara mentioned
earlier, Google is not

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:05.990
a very small company anymore.

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:08.958
It is a very large company
across all over the world,

00:17:08.958 --> 00:17:10.250
and offices all over the world.

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:13.060
And so my team does
collaborate with teams

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:15.040
that are located in the
different time zones.

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:17.880
And so part of the
frustration with that is,

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:20.490
the company is so
large that sometimes it

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:23.339
can be very difficult to know
what other parts of the company

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:24.670
are doing.

00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:28.410
So it's not at
all unusual for me

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:30.960
to first learn of
some Google products

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:33.870
when they show up
in Tech Crunch.

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:38.810
And it's just one of the facts
of being a large company.

00:17:38.810 --> 00:17:40.560
But I think that's
something I've actually

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:41.550
taken a bit to heart.

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:43.930
Because I work on
a G Suite product.

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:45.000
I work on meetings.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:47.260
I work on trying to
make that collaboration,

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:49.140
that connection better.

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:51.870
I work on trying to make
that more seamless, more

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:52.920
frictionless.

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:56.580
And so I think that that
kind of gripe with how large

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:59.293
a company is is something
that fuels me, as well.

00:17:59.293 --> 00:18:00.460
KRISTIN COPELAND: I love it.

00:18:00.460 --> 00:18:01.918
Tara, anything
you'd like to share?

00:18:01.918 --> 00:18:03.660
Should we move on?

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:04.742
All right, all right.

00:18:04.742 --> 00:18:06.700
TARA HERNANDEZ: I don't
want to get in trouble.

00:18:06.700 --> 00:18:08.910
KRISTIN COPELAND: One
of my favorite thing--

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:10.320
is it cause it's meetings?

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:11.760
You hate meetings the most?

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:12.500
OK.

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:13.625
TARA HERNANDEZ: No comment.

00:18:15.243 --> 00:18:17.160
KRISTIN COPELAND: One
of my favorite questions

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:21.040
to ask folks is, when
you're working at Google,

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:24.900
there's so many other companies
that you could choose from.

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:27.120
What brought you to Google?

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:28.685
And what's kept you here?

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:33.150
Tara, take it away.

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:35.040
TARA HERNANDEZ: All right.

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:38.010
I actually, like I
said, I had never

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:39.395
planned on working for Google.

00:18:39.395 --> 00:18:40.770
Not just because
of the interview

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:44.625
questions, but just because
I like small companies.

00:18:44.625 --> 00:18:46.500
The ability to have
impact at a small company

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:49.970
is just so much, to your
point, is just so much easier.

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:56.740
But I met some amazing women
at meet-ups and cloud events.

00:18:56.740 --> 00:19:01.140
And one in particular, a
woman named Melody McFessel.

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:03.420
Another one, Diane
Greene, Alyssa Greene.

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:05.130
There's just all of
these amazing women.

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:07.560
And I was like, wow,
here's a company

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:12.300
that has this sort of fulcrum
of people that I could really

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:15.600
learn from and have an
opportunity to work with.

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:17.370
And I thought, all
right, well, OK.

00:19:17.370 --> 00:19:19.680
Maybe I hate big companies,
but this is too good

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:21.720
an opportunity to pass up.

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:24.670
Plus they let me work in
the San Francisco office.

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:25.370
KRISTIN COPELAND: It's
very nice out here.

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:26.670
TARA HERNANDEZ: That
was an added benefit.

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:28.280
KRISTIN COPELAND: I like it.

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:29.230
Jimmy.

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:32.400
JIMMY MKUDE: So for me, if
you don't know my background,

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:33.700
I was born in Tanzania.

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:38.030
So growing up in kind of
a third world country,

00:19:38.030 --> 00:19:41.100
it was quite a change
of scenery for me

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:42.750
to come to the United States.

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:45.500
And through my
time in college, I

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:47.250
realized that I wanted
to dedicate my life

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:50.380
to solve real problems
that affect people.

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:54.120
And I didn't really care about
how many users you rack up

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:56.610
for all these different
companies we're talking about.

00:19:56.610 --> 00:20:00.270
But at Google, really
what drew me to Google

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:02.640
was the problem they're
trying to solve.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:05.920
First, they try to
affect the whole world.

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:09.730
So not just the United
States, or the first world.

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:11.430
They try and affect everywhere.

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:14.130
And their problems are
actually affecting people

00:20:14.130 --> 00:20:16.950
and enabling people to
do more with their lives,

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:18.420
and enjoy their time more.

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:20.440
So it's problems that
I really cared about.

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:22.080
So that's what
drew me to Google.

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:24.120
And what kept me at
Google, to be honest,

00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:26.440
I think it's the food.

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:28.135
The free food is
got to be up there.

00:20:28.135 --> 00:20:29.760
Like that is one of
my favorite things.

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:32.300
Every time I think of leaving,
I'm like, oh, but food.

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:33.720
OK, I'm just never mind.

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:34.720
KRISTIN COPELAND: Right.

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:35.860
We're very spoiled.

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:38.290
I'm sure most of you have
heard about the food.

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:40.770
But I can say that it's
one of those things.

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:43.980
I think I saw something like
a meme the other day that

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:45.620
was making fun of Googlers.

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:47.280
Who were talking
about like, I'd rather

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:49.775
work in the San Francisco
office because they have oat

00:20:49.775 --> 00:20:52.150
milk instead of almond milk.

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:54.960
And that's a little bit
painting the picture of how

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:57.840
spoiled we can be a little bit.

00:20:57.840 --> 00:20:59.590
TARA HERNANDEZ: New
York office has steak.

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:00.465
KRISTIN COPELAND: Oh.

00:21:00.465 --> 00:21:02.235
OK, well, I'm moving
to New York, clearly.

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:10.530
Tara, tell me about a little bit
more about what brought you--

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:13.290
sorry-- I'm going to move
on to Lakshmi because you

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:14.850
started that one for us.

00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:17.890
Clearly I'm just crushing
it right now, y'all.

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:19.800
Lakshmi, tell me what
brought you to Google

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:22.830
and what's kept you here.

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:26.730
LAKSHMI SHARMA: Like almost
everybody said that the--

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:29.190
like the impact, the
scale of the impact

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:32.950
that anybody can make, any
single person can make.

00:21:32.950 --> 00:21:36.180
So I'm in networking,
networking infrastructure.

00:21:36.180 --> 00:21:39.390
So anything, everything
is connected and secured

00:21:39.390 --> 00:21:41.040
through some kind of networking.

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:43.350
All the way from their
device to on prem device,

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:46.790
to a datacenter, to cloud.

00:21:46.790 --> 00:21:48.660
And this is a company
which has built it

00:21:48.660 --> 00:21:52.980
over years and securely
and phenomenally well.

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:55.560
I think it was a perfect timing,
because we are not number

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:57.350
one yet in cloud game.

00:21:57.350 --> 00:22:00.895
So I had huge scope to
kind of participate.

00:22:00.895 --> 00:22:02.520
And at the scale that
I was looking at,

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:05.572
there is no other place I
could have thought about where

00:22:05.572 --> 00:22:06.530
I could have an impact.

00:22:06.530 --> 00:22:08.850
Where there was so
much to do at the scale

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:11.550
that they had already done.

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:13.110
And what is keeping me?

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:15.270
Just that even after
15 months, everyday I

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:17.430
feel like I'm a Newgler.

00:22:17.430 --> 00:22:19.870
It is so much to do.

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:22.320
So that's what is
my promise to myself

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:24.480
that I will be able
to make an impact,

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:27.570
and will be able to work
with really smart brains.

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:29.968
Every single day I meet
somebody, I'm like, oh, my God.

00:22:29.968 --> 00:22:31.260
Oh, this person wrote the book.

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:32.880
Oh, my God, this
person did this.

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:34.620
So it's just everyday
those moments.

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:36.570
And then learning from
them and being humble,

00:22:36.570 --> 00:22:38.050
that's keeping me here.

00:22:38.050 --> 00:22:39.425
KRISTIN COPELAND:
That's amazing.

00:22:39.425 --> 00:22:42.178
So speaking of the smart people
that you come in contact with.

00:22:42.178 --> 00:22:43.470
TARA HERNANDEZ: One more thing.

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:44.580
The thing that
keeps me at Google,

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:45.913
we can bring dogs to the office.

00:22:45.913 --> 00:22:47.310
KRISTIN COPELAND: You can what?

00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:49.018
TARA HERNANDEZ: Bring
dogs to the office.

00:22:49.018 --> 00:22:49.620
The dogs.

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:51.210
Puppies in the
office is literally

00:22:51.210 --> 00:22:54.090
one of my favorite
things about Google.

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:57.940
I can't even handle
the cuteness some days.

00:22:57.940 --> 00:23:01.020
Two of my co-workers have the
cutest puppies I've ever seen.

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:02.610
I'm so sorry to get
on this tangent,

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:06.030
but yes, dogs in the
office is amazing.

00:23:06.030 --> 00:23:07.590
But you did touch
on something that I

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:08.882
want to dig a little deeper on.

00:23:08.882 --> 00:23:14.610
The point of working with people
who are just so intelligent.

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:16.240
I'll give you my
quick experience.

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:18.840
So I was leading a meeting,
and one of those meetings

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:21.792
where like I kind of
felt like, I got this.

00:23:21.792 --> 00:23:22.750
I'm going to nail this.

00:23:22.750 --> 00:23:26.940
I went into the meeting
all strong and confident,

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:28.260
felt like I rocked the meeting.

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:30.390
And then afterwards,
someone was like,

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:33.920
did you know that that guy in
the meeting with you, he like

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:35.870
was a bad ass at Atari.

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:39.590
He was one of those people
that made huge impact, built

00:23:39.590 --> 00:23:41.420
all these incredible games.

00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:44.660
And you just made
sure that he knew

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:46.140
exactly what he needed to do.

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:47.200
And he's super senior.

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:49.700
And then all of a sudden the
next time I'm meeting with him,

00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:53.150
I'm sitting there like,
oh, this guy's so smart.

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:55.580
I'm going to look like
an idiot in front of him.

00:23:55.580 --> 00:23:57.710
So it gave me a little
bit of imposter syndrome

00:23:57.710 --> 00:24:00.980
of what it's like to work
with these crazy intelligent,

00:24:00.980 --> 00:24:02.240
amazing people.

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:04.070
And I'd love to hear
a little bit more

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:07.910
about if you experience
imposter syndrome at Google.

00:24:07.910 --> 00:24:10.087
Or how you deal with it.

00:24:10.087 --> 00:24:12.170
LAKSHMI SHARMA: I think
this is one of the reasons

00:24:12.170 --> 00:24:13.010
that I did not--

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:14.900
Google had been my
customer, partner,

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:19.460
I'd worked with many
times, and vendor.

00:24:19.460 --> 00:24:21.740
But I never wanted
to work with Google.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:24.050
Of the reasons was
that hey, from outside

00:24:24.050 --> 00:24:26.570
it looks like when there are
so many smart people, who

00:24:26.570 --> 00:24:29.390
each have so many things
in life, the impression

00:24:29.390 --> 00:24:32.480
that you feel like, if I
am there, one I'll be lost.

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:34.850
Whether I'll be able
to make an impact.

00:24:34.850 --> 00:24:38.210
And second is that, oh, where
do I get the kind of respect

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:40.910
that I get at other the places?

00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:43.430
Experience internally
is just like you said.

00:24:43.430 --> 00:24:44.860
So I'll give a
very quick example.

00:24:44.860 --> 00:24:46.800
As I grew up in India.

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:49.400
I did my Master's in
computer science from India.

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:51.560
And there's a very common
book that lot of Indians

00:24:51.560 --> 00:24:53.910
read all the time like
in Master's which is

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:55.590
around computer architecture.

00:24:55.590 --> 00:24:57.680
So the person, one of
the authors of that book,

00:24:57.680 --> 00:24:59.720
I have been working
with this person

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:01.580
ever since I joined last year.

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:05.240
Just recently another person
who joined my team again,

00:25:05.240 --> 00:25:07.750
this person is Indian,
says, oh, Lakshmi.

00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:09.980
Isn't he [INAUDIBLE]?

00:25:09.980 --> 00:25:10.947
So I said, yeah.

00:25:10.947 --> 00:25:13.280
So he said, isn't he the
[INAUDIBLE] who wrote the book?

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:14.960
I'm like, [GASPS] what book?

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:16.550
So then I actually look at him.

00:25:16.550 --> 00:25:18.530
I said, David, did
you write that book?

00:25:18.530 --> 00:25:19.400
He said, yeah.

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:21.780
And I'm just going
like, oh, my God.

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:23.510
It's been 13 months,
and I'm working

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:25.250
with this person every day.

00:25:25.250 --> 00:25:28.220
And I read his book,
and almost every person

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:32.450
at least 18 years back in
India used to read that book.

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:33.830
So that's the kind
of humbleness.

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:35.360
And I can give names and names.

00:25:35.360 --> 00:25:39.710
Like person who wrote TCP,
Vint's, like amazing person.

00:25:39.710 --> 00:25:42.590
I got to meet that
person with no air.

00:25:42.590 --> 00:25:46.610
Everybody here is just here
to solve problems for people.

00:25:46.610 --> 00:25:50.970
And that's a common thing
that keeps us all connected.

00:25:50.970 --> 00:25:52.280
And that's all we do.

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:55.640
I just get goosebumps, because
I am engineer at heart.

00:25:55.640 --> 00:25:56.580
I'm a product manager.

00:25:56.580 --> 00:25:59.100
But meeting those people
who are so humble.

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:02.620
And then is just--

00:26:02.620 --> 00:26:03.383
it's just amazing

00:26:03.383 --> 00:26:04.550
KRISTIN COPELAND: I love it.

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:06.107
Go ahead, Tara.

00:26:06.107 --> 00:26:07.940
TARA HERNANDEZ: So I've
actually given talks

00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:10.642
on imposter syndrome, and
I suffer from it every day.

00:26:10.642 --> 00:26:12.350
And the thing that I
love about at Google

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:14.490
is that so does everybody else.

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:17.188
It's something that
gets talked about a lot.

00:26:17.188 --> 00:26:19.730
And you think about, I'm sure
everybody's heard of the Google

00:26:19.730 --> 00:26:21.110
20% projects.

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:22.550
And that's pretty widely known.

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:26.090
There was somebody who had a
20% project and invented Gmail.

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:29.777
And it just passed 15 years,
and has like 90 bazillion users.

00:26:29.777 --> 00:26:30.860
And you think, oh, my God.

00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:33.380
I'm working at a company where
someone just thought of that

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:34.950
as a side project.

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:36.530
That's very intimidating.

00:26:36.530 --> 00:26:40.010
But at the same time,
to Lakshmi's point,

00:26:40.010 --> 00:26:41.660
there's a lot of
motivation around.

00:26:41.660 --> 00:26:44.120
We all have the
capability and opportunity

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:46.160
to really do
something meaningful

00:26:46.160 --> 00:26:47.840
for us and for the company.

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:51.902
And more to Jimmy's point,
to the world at large.

00:26:51.902 --> 00:26:54.110
It's not often that you get
to work at a company that

00:26:54.110 --> 00:26:55.310
could have global impact.

00:26:55.310 --> 00:26:57.050
And so there's a sense
of responsibility

00:26:57.050 --> 00:27:01.250
that I think a lot of
us carry that maybe it's

00:27:01.250 --> 00:27:04.610
not the official motto, but
every employee is, do good,

00:27:04.610 --> 00:27:06.500
don't be evil.

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:09.230
Don't take it for granted
All of those things

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:13.762
that for us as Googlers in
the trenches working along,

00:27:13.762 --> 00:27:15.470
it's like we think
about it all the time.

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:15.610
KRISTIN COPELAND: Yeah.

00:27:15.610 --> 00:27:16.360
Love it.

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:18.080
TARA HERNANDEZ: So
intent in some ways,

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:21.980
and a sense of sort
of technical ethics,

00:27:21.980 --> 00:27:23.480
and other types of
things are things

00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:25.550
that are just as
important as what

00:27:25.550 --> 00:27:27.122
type of crazy
technical innovation

00:27:27.122 --> 00:27:28.580
is going to make
us a lot of money.

00:27:28.580 --> 00:27:30.455
That's not really what
we care about as much.

00:27:30.455 --> 00:27:32.960
At least with all of the
people that I have met,

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:34.190
that's not the case.

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:36.110
KRISTIN COPELAND: Absolutely.

00:27:36.110 --> 00:27:40.610
I'd love to hear a little
bit more about skills

00:27:40.610 --> 00:27:42.020
that you've developed at Google.

00:27:42.020 --> 00:27:44.150
Because I know I
personally have seen

00:27:44.150 --> 00:27:47.000
some of these different courses
we have available to us,

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:48.450
and have benefited from them.

00:27:48.450 --> 00:27:52.310
I recently took a finance
course which is amazing,

00:27:52.310 --> 00:27:56.157
because let's be real, I know
nothing about how to invest.

00:27:56.157 --> 00:27:56.990
So I'd love to hear.

00:27:56.990 --> 00:27:59.157
What's a skill that you've
developed here at Google?

00:27:59.157 --> 00:28:01.910
Whether or not it's related
to your core job, or something

00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:04.260
that you've learned via
courses that we offer.

00:28:04.260 --> 00:28:04.760
Share.

00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:06.010
MARISA CHOW: So I'll list two.

00:28:06.010 --> 00:28:09.800
One technical skill and then
one non-technical skill.

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:12.770
Technical skill is, so my team
works on video conferencing,

00:28:12.770 --> 00:28:13.670
as I mentioned.

00:28:13.670 --> 00:28:15.710
And a large part of
that is reliability,

00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:18.290
so making sure that our
rooms are reliably working

00:28:18.290 --> 00:28:19.890
at any given point in time.

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:23.420
So a huge part of
my job is looking

00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:29.420
at rooms that are broken, trying
to diagnose why something is--

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:32.300
why a room is
suddenly went offline.

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:35.107
And there is just so many
dimensions to that problem.

00:28:35.107 --> 00:28:36.440
Is it a problem in the software?

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:37.550
Is there a problem
in the hardware?

00:28:37.550 --> 00:28:39.008
Is there a problem
in the building?

00:28:41.210 --> 00:28:46.033
There are layers and layers of
where the problem could happen.

00:28:46.033 --> 00:28:47.450
So I would say one
skill that I've

00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:50.720
developed is learning
to look at a symptom

00:28:50.720 --> 00:28:52.970
and try to think about
what are the causes that

00:28:52.970 --> 00:28:55.400
could affect that, that
could make that happen?

00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:58.520
Where along the chain
could something go wrong?

00:28:58.520 --> 00:29:02.090
The non-technical
skill is really fun.

00:29:02.090 --> 00:29:05.210
I took an ASL class
at the office.

00:29:05.210 --> 00:29:08.700
They offer ASL classes.

00:29:08.700 --> 00:29:12.680
And it was something I'd
always wanted to learn,

00:29:12.680 --> 00:29:15.323
and didn't really have
a chance to learn.

00:29:15.323 --> 00:29:17.490
So when they offered the
class, I just jumped at it.

00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:18.990
And it was a lot of fun.

00:29:18.990 --> 00:29:19.823
I really enjoyed it.

00:29:19.823 --> 00:29:21.115
KRISTIN COPELAND: That's great.

00:29:21.115 --> 00:29:22.070
Anyone else?

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:23.778
JIMMY MKUDE: So the
skill that I learned,

00:29:23.778 --> 00:29:26.720
actually it's weird because
I thought I was the master

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:28.110
of C++.

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:30.890
I was like there's nothing
I can learn more about C++.

00:29:30.890 --> 00:29:32.540
TARA HERNANDEZ: [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:32.540 --> 00:29:34.410
JIMMY MKUDE: That's
what I learned.

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:36.590
So at Google we have
this thing where

00:29:36.590 --> 00:29:38.690
after you join the
company, you can

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:40.550
go through readability reviews.

00:29:40.550 --> 00:29:43.280
And you have people who are very
knowledgeable in the language

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:46.550
review your code and give
you pointers to what you do.

00:29:46.550 --> 00:29:49.280
And my first readability
approval review,

00:29:49.280 --> 00:29:52.490
that was the most exciting
moment I ever had.

00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:55.850
Because I learned so
much in that one review,

00:29:55.850 --> 00:29:57.650
than let's say, a
year in college.

00:29:57.650 --> 00:30:03.373
Because gave me pointers
and reasons and why--

00:30:03.373 --> 00:30:04.790
like you give the
whole rationale.

00:30:04.790 --> 00:30:06.502
Do it like this because of ABC.

00:30:06.502 --> 00:30:08.210
And you think about
it, you're like, yes.

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:09.627
Why didn't I think
of that before?

00:30:09.627 --> 00:30:10.910
And you go back and it's--

00:30:10.910 --> 00:30:13.070
every time I have those
reviews, it' just amazing.

00:30:13.070 --> 00:30:14.630
And these people
work in the company,

00:30:14.630 --> 00:30:16.667
and you can just ping
them and be like,

00:30:16.667 --> 00:30:17.750
do you want to have lunch?

00:30:17.750 --> 00:30:18.470
And they'll be like, yeah.

00:30:18.470 --> 00:30:19.180
Let's get lunch.

00:30:19.180 --> 00:30:20.180
We have steak over here.

00:30:20.180 --> 00:30:21.740
So you go and talk to them.

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:23.180
And you learn so--

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:27.137
I am a C++ nerd so I enjoy
having lunch with these people.

00:30:27.137 --> 00:30:28.970
And the more I learned,
the more happy I am.

00:30:28.970 --> 00:30:31.880
So I don't think I'm ever
going to be a master at C++.

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:34.300
That's what I learned, as well.

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:35.373
Be humble.

00:30:35.373 --> 00:30:36.540
KRISTIN COPELAND: I love it.

00:30:36.540 --> 00:30:38.100
I love it.

00:30:38.100 --> 00:30:40.680
So in our last minute or
so, we'll end on a fun note.

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:43.660
I'd love for each of you-- one
of you mentioned the great food

00:30:43.660 --> 00:30:44.660
at Google.

00:30:44.660 --> 00:30:46.590
I'd love to hear
from each of you

00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:50.840
what the best thing
you've eaten at Google is.

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:52.600
The best food you've
eaten at Google.

00:30:52.600 --> 00:30:53.100
Yeah.

00:30:56.200 --> 00:30:57.180
All right so mine is--

00:30:57.180 --> 00:30:58.888
JIMMY MKUDE: It's got
to be Japan office.

00:30:58.888 --> 00:31:01.020
In Tokyo, they had--

00:31:01.020 --> 00:31:03.660
I don't know what the name of
the food is, but it was just,

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:05.160
it's different from
here because you

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:10.440
have sort of just one meal
is little meals everywhere.

00:31:10.440 --> 00:31:12.457
And you just pick
and choose and eat.

00:31:12.457 --> 00:31:14.540
And I remember eating, the
first time I was there,

00:31:14.540 --> 00:31:16.560
I ate so much that I got sick.

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:17.703
But it was so good.

00:31:17.703 --> 00:31:18.870
KRISTIN COPELAND: I love it.

00:31:18.870 --> 00:31:20.370
JIMMY MKUDE: It
was so different.

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:23.430
KRISTIN COPELAND: Like a
Japanese version of tapas?

00:31:23.430 --> 00:31:24.030
OK.

00:31:24.030 --> 00:31:26.040
Yeah, I can get on
board with that.

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:27.400
Lakshmi, what about you?

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:29.550
LAKSHMI SHARMA: So I'm
vegetarian, no egg,

00:31:29.550 --> 00:31:32.670
and no meat, no seafood.

00:31:32.670 --> 00:31:34.920
So one thing which I really
love is that the food here

00:31:34.920 --> 00:31:35.940
is super healthy.

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:37.800
And I do like healthy food.

00:31:37.800 --> 00:31:39.340
So you have all
kinds of options,

00:31:39.340 --> 00:31:42.390
but out of all the
offices I've travelled,

00:31:42.390 --> 00:31:47.190
Google at Singapore office, they
have like a seven star buffet.

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:49.470
And the view is
so super awesome,

00:31:49.470 --> 00:31:53.620
so that's my favorite
place for vegetarian food.

00:31:53.620 --> 00:31:54.990
KRISTIN COPELAND: Awesome.

00:31:54.990 --> 00:31:55.800
All right, Tara.

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:56.700
What about you?

00:31:56.700 --> 00:31:57.600
TARA HERNANDEZ:
Chocolate chip cookies

00:31:57.600 --> 00:31:59.160
at the San Francisco office.

00:31:59.160 --> 00:32:02.560
KRISTIN COPELAND: Yes,
those are amazing.

00:32:02.560 --> 00:32:05.730
TARA HERNANDEZ: They
have sea salt, and--

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:07.920
MARISA CHOW: I feel like
my answer to this changes

00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:10.680
every week.

00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:15.998
But yes, so in the
New York office

00:32:15.998 --> 00:32:18.540
they have this one cafe where
they will plate everything very

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:20.130
nicely on like a huge plate.

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:22.010
So you see this
beautiful white plate,

00:32:22.010 --> 00:32:28.000
and it'll have like a
perfect I don't know--

